––– comment –––
A two-sided string range is one condition inside bool should and must_not, as an array item or an object.
––– input –––
q() { curl -sS -X POST http://localhost:9308/search -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d "{\"table\":\"gh5006\",\"query\":{\"bool\":$1},\"sort\":[{\"id\":\"asc\"}],\"limit\":20}" | php -r '$r=json_decode(stream_get_contents(STDIN),true); echo json_encode(array_column($r["hits"]["hits"],"_id")),PHP_EOL;'; }
R='{"range":{"rs":{"gt":"a","lt":"n"}}}'
q "{\"should\":[$R]}"
q "{\"must_not\":[$R]}"
q "{\"must_not\":$R}"
––– output –––
[3,4,5]
[1,2,6,7,8]
[1,2,6,7,8]
––– comment –––
Equality and IN must still use secondary indexes. SHOW META verifies the actual row iterator,
so this control cannot pass merely because COUNT(*) used its precalculation shortcut.
––– input –––
for attr in rs cs; do
  printf '%s equality and IN:\n' "$attr"
  sql "SELECT id FROM gh5006 WHERE $attr='m' ORDER BY id ASC /*+ SecondaryIndex($attr) */; SHOW META LIKE 'index'; SELECT id FROM gh5006 WHERE $attr IN ('m','z') ORDER BY id ASC /*+ SecondaryIndex($attr) */; SHOW META LIKE 'index';" | tr '\t' ' '
done
––– output –––
rs equality and IN:
4
5
index rs:SecondaryIndex (100%)
4
5
8
index rs:SecondaryIndex (100%)
cs equality and IN:
4
5
index cs:SecondaryIndex (100%)
4
5
8
index cs:SecondaryIndex (100%)
––– comment –––
Equality and its excluded form must retain their distinct COUNT(*) results.
––– input –––
for attr in rs cs; do
  printf '%s equality/inequality counts: ' "$attr"
  sql "SELECT COUNT(*) FROM gh5006 WHERE $attr='m'; SELECT COUNT(*) FROM gh5006 WHERE $attr!='m';" | paste -sd, -
done
––– output –––
rs equality/inequality counts: 2,6
cs equality/inequality counts: 2,6
––– comment –––
A range remains a residual filter beside a supported secondary index on another attribute.
The matching cases also verify index use; the nonmatching cases ensure the range is not dropped.
––– input –––
sql "SELECT id FROM gh5006 WHERE rs>'m' AND cs='z' ORDER BY id ASC /*+ SecondaryIndex(cs) */; SHOW META LIKE 'index'; SELECT id FROM gh5006 WHERE cs>'m' AND rs='z' ORDER BY id ASC /*+ SecondaryIndex(rs) */; SHOW META LIKE 'index'; SELECT COUNT(*) FROM gh5006 WHERE rs<'m' AND cs='z' /*+ SecondaryIndex(cs) */; SELECT COUNT(*) FROM gh5006 WHERE cs<'m' AND rs='z' /*+ SecondaryIndex(rs) */;" | tr '\t' ' '
––– output –––
8
index cs:SecondaryIndex (100%)
8
index rs:SecondaryIndex (100%)
0
0
––– comment –––
A supported columnar equality analyzer must still work beside a residual string range.
––– input –––
sql "SELECT id FROM gh5006 WHERE rs>'a' AND cs='m' ORDER BY id ASC /*+ ColumnarScan(cs) */; SHOW META LIKE 'index'; SELECT COUNT(*) FROM gh5006 WHERE rs<'a' AND cs='m' /*+ ColumnarScan(cs) */;" | tr '\t' ' '
––– output –––
4
5
index $cs_HASH:ColumnarScan (100%)
0
––– comment –––
A string range on an expression key (not an attribute) keeps its direction; concat(rs,cs) doubles the value.
––– input –––
q '{"must":[{"range":{"concat(rs,cs)":{"gt":"mm"}}}]}'
––– output –––
[6,7,8]
––– comment –––
Single-filter COUNT(*) precalculation hashes strings with the default collation, so it must not
answer string filters under a case-sensitive session collation (no row holds 'M').
––– input –––
sql "SET collation_connection=binary; SELECT COUNT(*) FROM gh5006 WHERE rs='M'; SELECT COUNT(*) FROM gh5006 WHERE cs IN ('M','z'); SELECT COUNT(*) FROM gh5006 WHERE j.s='M';" | paste -sd, -
––– output –––
0,1,0
––– comment –––
The query cache must keep string comparison directions apart: every query below has the same
MATCH() and string, so a cached result of one direction must not be served for another.
––– input –––
sql "SET GLOBAL qcache_thresh_msec=0; CREATE TABLE gh5006qc (f TEXT, s STRING); INSERT INTO gh5006qc VALUES (1,'x','a'),(2,'x','m'),(3,'x','z'); FLUSH RAMCHUNK gh5006qc;"
for op in '>' '<' '=' '>=' '<=' '!='; do
  printf "s %s 'm': " "$op"
  sql "SELECT id FROM gh5006qc WHERE MATCH('x') AND s $op 'm' ORDER BY id ASC;" | paste -sd, -
done
––– output –––
s > 'm': 3
s < 'm': 1
s = 'm': 2
s >= 'm': 2,3
s <= 'm': 1,2
s != 'm': 1,3
––– input –––
sql "DROP TABLE gh5006; DROP TABLE gh5006qc;"
––– output –––
